Maya Chen: Heads-up 1 I'm doing a pass to align all streaming + pagination samples across py/ts/go. Found a couple inconsistencies and want to coordinate the PR so reviews are quick.

Ravi Desai: thanks. which inconsistencies?

Maya Chen: summary: (1) python examples sometimes show sync client for streaming which is wrongwe want async-only examples for stream(), (2) TypeScript snippets don't demonstrate AbortSignal cancellation, (3) Go pagination examples assume nil cursor API but some clients return empty string, (4) several internal links point to the old /models page.

Docs CI: lint report queued for branch "docs/sdk-snippets-unify" once PR is opened.

Elena Popov: on (1) I ran into the sync/async mixup too. Please show both async generator and context-manager pattern for closing streams. Short snippet is better than long explanation.

Maya Chen: agreed. Python snippet I plan to add (async generator + context manager) and also a short sync alternative for teams using sync SDK with chunked polling.

Ravi Desai: for TS aborts, we should show two patterns: using AbortController for manual cancel and an auto-timeout helper. Helps when customers want predictable token billing.

Maya Chen: adding that. snippet draft for TS:

```ts
// manual cancellation
const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);
for await (const chunk of await client.responses.stream({ input: '..' }, { signal: controller.signal })) {
  handle(chunk);
}

// auto timeout helper
await withTimeout(10_000, async () => {
  for await (const chunk of await client.responses.stream({ input: '..' })) handle(chunk);
});
```

Oliver West: small nit on API surface: the TS stream helper takes options in second arg in some versions, in others it's merged. Can you add a compatibility note so examples don't break for older pinned SDKs?

Maya Chen: will add a short compatibility paragraph and link to SDK change log. Also adding explicit comment about stream backpressure and not buffering entire stream in memory.

Elena Popov: Go pagination example: show both nil and empty string handling, and a defensive check for zero-length page to avoid infinite loops when API returns same cursor back. Something like:

```go
cursor := ""
for {
    resp, err := client.ListEmbeddings(ctx, &api.ListParams{Cursor: cursor, Limit: 200})
    if err != nil { return err }
    if len(resp.Items) == 0 && resp.NextCursor == cursor { break }
    for _, it := range resp.Items { process(it) }
    if resp.NextCursor == "" || resp.NextCursor == nil { break }
    cursor = resp.NextCursor
}
```

Ravi Desai: FYI please avoid "nil" literal in docs, show the language-idiomatic checking (e.g., resp.NextCursor == "" || resp.NextCursor == nil is confusing in Go example). Prefer showing empty-string check and then mention older SDKs may return nil depending on language binding.

Maya Chen: good point, I'll simplify Go example to empty-string check and call out older bindings in a footnote.

